Step 1: Open the terminal in VS Code on your workstation (Ctrl+backtick)

  Step 2: Navigate to wherever the SAS files are. If it's a network drive like S:\ or \\server\share\, just cd to it:

  # PowerShell
  cd S:\
  # or
  cd "\\server\share\analytics"

  Step 3: Run this (PowerShell):

  Get-ChildItem -Path . -Filter "*.sas" -Recurse | Select-String -Pattern "FTH|MOM|ESV|MRF|NMI|RSS|TAO|RMG|VCN|IRI|O2P|C
  RV|IDE|ZHR|PFS|PRA|DAR|VSX|RCR|COB|VAW|GNE|COR|VUI|PVE|MOS|RAT|MFY|MMT|IFC" | Out-File
  "$HOME\Desktop\mnemonic_matches.txt"

  Or if you have cmd:

  findstr /s /i /n "FTH MOM ESV MRF NMI RSS TAO RMG VCN IRI O2P CRV IDE ZHR PFS PRA DAR VSX RCR COB VAW GNE COR VUI PVE
  MOS RAT MFY MMT IFC" *.sas > %USERPROFILE%\Desktop\mnemonic_matches.txt

  That dumps everything to a file on your desktop. Then drop that file into this repo and I'll parse the whole thing —
  map every hit to the 31 campaigns and tell you which files have the success logic for each one.

  The search will be slow if it's over the network, but it only needs to run once. Let it churn.

❯ I need to type the literal path what is the command for CD literal path

● For a UNC path (the \\server\share kind), cd doesn't work directly in PowerShell or cmd. Use:

  PowerShell:
  Set-Location -LiteralPath "\\server\share\folder"

  Or map it to a drive first:
  New-PSDrive -Name X -PSProvider FileSystem -Root "\\server\share\folder"
  cd X:\

  cmd:
  pushd "\\server\share\folder"

  pushd in cmd automatically maps a temporary drive letter for UNC paths — it's the easiest option if you're using cmd.
